   "note": "STAGE 1 -- run the A-series at DECOMP alongside the usual P00 reference and TWIN_DECOMP twin, and gate each A-template against P00. An A-template whose floor_ratio <= 1.00 means P00 was ALREADY converged in that knob: the extra work bought nothing measurable and that template is pure added cost, never an accuracy improvement. STAGE 2 -- for whichever A-template survives with the LARGEST drift (the biggest real correction), re-run fidelity_gate.py using its own wrfout as --reference. Only then is every other candidate's nd an error against a better-converged solution rather than a distance from P00, which is what the accuracy ranking above actually wants to measure. Until stage 2 exists, treat the cost-lever ranking as 'least departure from the current operating point', not as 'most accurate'. The user makes the final decision -- this tool only narrows the search."
  },
  "decomposition_note": "DECOMP=C13 (this run's own STRICT top-1 shortlist entry) is unchanged from perf mode: STRICT decomposition knobs (nproc_x/nproc_y/numtiles/OMP/distribution) redistribute the same arithmetic and never change the physics, so they are a FREE AXIS in accuracy mode -- the fastest legal decomposition is also the most accurate one. They do set the chaos floor every fidelity number below is judged against, which is the opposite of being a fidelity lever: a decomposition change is the NOISE, not the signal."
 },
